================================================================================
Part 1: A1.5, the K6 syscall census
================================================================================

1. Is the committed log real?
-----------------------------
  $ gh run view --job 128002974256 --log > api.log     # the census job
  $ diff <(sed -E 's/\r$//' api.log) <(sed -E 's/\r$//' docs/logs/k6-syscall-census-42250209391.log) | wc -l
  0

1067 lines on both sides, identical. The file is the unedited job log of the
census job in run 42250209391, which is the mathd run for 0be000e:

  $ gh run view 42250209391 --json jobs
  test (windows-latest) success 128002973965
  k6 syscall census (strace musl) success 128002974256
  test (x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu) success 128002974271
  test (x86_64-unknown-linux-musl) success 128002974281

2. What the job does, and whether the oracle is strace
------------------------------------------------------
The job compiles the musl test binaries without running them
(`cargo test --no-run --message-format=json --target x86_64-unknown-linux-musl`),
then runs each binary under `strace -f -c -o summary_file <binary>` and parses
the count tables. strace is the oracle: the counts are kernel entries, not the
program's own claims. The log lists the 11 binaries it found and a table per
binary:

  Found 11 test binaries for x86_64-unknown-linux-musl:
    - mathd-a6d805dfd52976ef ... mathd_cli-d6812994886b6eb9 ...
    - ledger-d1d11824737aa32a, differentiator-2f05f171fed5a450,
      evaluator-221fe7eb2233000a, card_store-2a364b262cfe515d,
      canonical-68b40feeeb73cae0, verifier-19a362f9f82f06c3,
      roundtrip-cb11d67841acb92f, corpus-7811963f660b86c4
  ... 11 tables, each headed "% time  seconds  usecs/call  calls  errors syscall"

3. Do the displayed counts match the data?
------------------------------------------
From the combined table in the log, parsed back out of the pasted text:

  types listed: 33   sum of the 33 call counts: 74216
  Total syscall types:                33
  Total syscall calls:             74216

The 33 listed rows sum exactly to the printed total. The classification also
matches the document it claims to compare against: `docs/k6-design.md` lines
54-69 name read, write, open, close, mmap, munmap, brk, exit, arch_prctl,
set_tid_address, clock_gettime, exit_group (12), and the census prints
9 [MATCH] + 3 [UNEXERCISED] = 12, with clock_gettime, exit and exit_group
unexercised. The 4 [FALLBACK] entries are read from the same doc's fallback list
(ioctl, fstat, mprotect, getrandom were called; newfstatat and prlimit64 were
not), and the 20 [NEW/EXTRA] entries are all really outside that list.

4. The plant: does the census notice a failing test binary?
----------------------------------------------------------
This is the question that decides whether the numbers mean anything. Branch
`plant/f1.9-census-green`, one file `mathd/tests/alway_failing_plant.rs` with a
single test that always panics:

  $ gh run view 42254970064 --json jobs
  k6 syscall census (strace musl) success
  test (windows-latest) failure
  test (x86_64-unknown-linux-musl) failure
  test (x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu) failure

The census job printed the failure and ignored it:

  Test binary alway_failing_plant-40b058aab20617c3 exited with code 101
  test plant_always_fails ... FAILED
  test result: FAILED. 0 passed; 1 failed; 0 ignored; 0 measured; 0 filtered out
  Total syscall types:                33
  Total syscall calls:             85965

FINDING C1 (blocking for the census tool, not for the gate). The census job is
green even when every test binary fails. The script prints the non-zero exit
status to stderr and never exits non-zero itself; the comparison section sets no
failure condition at all. So a suite that panics early still produces a census,
with smaller counts, and nothing says so. The three test jobs caught this plant
because they assert on test results; the census job does not.

5. Findings and notes on A1.5
-----------------------------
C2 (unexercised exit/exit_group is unexplained). The census reports exit and
exit_group as never called. A1.5's team message explains it as "process
termination does not return to strace -c counter". That is an assertion, not a
measurement, and the log shows something stronger: none of the 11 strace tables
contains an exit or exit_group row at all, which is not what a plain
`strace -c <binary>` usually shows. Either strace omits never-returning calls in
this summary mode or the parse dropped the row; the census does not say, and it
has no test that would notice. For A2's K6 work the distinction matters: "not
exercised" here means "not seen by this measurement", not "not needed".
C2b (clock_gettime). Its absence is consistent with the vDSO (a library call
served in user space never enters the kernel), so "clock_gettime is not
exercised" is expected for a normal musl binary and does not mean A2.26 can skip
the syscall: a kernel without CLOCK_MONOTONIC in syscall form would still break
any binary that falls back. A1.5 does not mention the vDSO; the conclusion
posted to A2 should.
C3 (paths filter). The census job inherits the workflow's `paths` filter
(mathd/** and the workflow file only), so a change to rust-toolchain.toml does
not refresh the census. Same family as the finding in
docs/reviews/mathd-matrix-freebuff.log; one fix covers both.
C4 (counts include harness noise, and the log says so). futex alone is 28,827 of
74,216 calls, clone/gettid/sigaltstack/rt_sigprocmask are the Rust test harness
threading, and execve is strace's own child launch. A1.5 says this in the team
message; the log itself does not annotate it, which is fine, but a reader picking
up the log alone could take 33 as "Nucleus must implement 33 syscalls". The 12
core + 4 fallback split is the number to use.

VERDICT for A1.5: PASS on the census itself. The log is unedited, the oracle is
strace, the table sums to the printed total, and the comparison against
docs/k6-design.md is correct with the 12 names verified against the document.
Two things to fix in the job, not in the census data: C1 (it must fail when a
test binary fails, or the numbers silently shrink) and the exits/clock_gettime
explanation (C2) before A2 relies on those three "unexercised" entries.

